{"name":"Unripe Banana","hex":"#f0ff57","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"unripe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Not ripe.","Developing too early; premature."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/unripe"},{"term":"banana","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The elongated curved tropical fruit of a banana plant, which grows in bunches and has a creamy flesh and a smooth skin. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","In particular, the sweet, yellow fruit of the Cavendish banana cultivar, which may be eaten raw, as distinct from e.g. a plantain for cooking."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Curved like a banana, especially of a ball in flight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/banana"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ripening","blocks":[{"text":"Ripening is a process in fruits that causes them to become more palatable. In general, fruit becomes sweeter, less green, and softer as it ripens. Even though the acidity of fruit increases as it ripens, the higher acidity level does not make the fruit seem tarter. This effect is attributed to the Brix-Acid Ratio. Climacteric fruits ripen after harvesting and so some fruits for market are picked green."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Veraison_Cabernet_Sauvignon.jpg/330px-Veraison_Cabernet_Sauvignon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ripening"},{"title":"Banana","blocks":[{"text":"A banana is an elongated, edible fruit that is botanically a berry produced by several kinds of large treelike herbaceous flowering plants in the genus Musa. In some countries, cooking bananas are called plantains, distinguishing them from dessert bananas. The fruit is variable in size, color and firmness, but is usually elongated and curved, with soft flesh rich in starch covered with a peel, which may have a variety of colors when ripe. It grows upward in clusters near the top of the plant. Almost all modern edible seedless (parthenocarp) cultivated bananas come from two wild species – Musa acuminata and Musa balbisiana, or their hybrids."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Bananavarieties.jpg/330px-Bananavarieties.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banana"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Methylcyclopropene.png/330px-Methylcyclopropene.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Methylcyclopropene.png/330px-Methylcyclopropene.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"1‑Methylcyclopropene is used as a synthetic plant growth regulator.[2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMethylcyclopropene.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/2017-10-26_Ripening_lemons_on_a_tree%2C_Albufeira_%282%29.JPG/500px-2017-10-26_Ripening_lemons_on_a_tree%2C_Albufeira_%282%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/2017-10-26_Ripening_lemons_on_a_tree%2C_Albufeira_%282%29.JPG/1280px-2017-10-26_Ripening_lemons_on_a_tree%2C_Albufeira_%282%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/2017-10-26_Ripening_lemons_on_a_tree%2C_Albufeira_%282%29.JPG/500px-2017-10-26_Ripening_lemons_on_a_tree%2C_Albufeira_%282%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lemons turn yellow as they ripen.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2017-10-26_Ripening_lemons_on_a_tree%2C_Albufeira_(2).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Grape_tomato_cluster_partial